-- title:
--   Spider process $S_t=\theta_{m(t)}|B_t|$, its ribs, the British-rail diameter $D_t$ and its stopping times
-- statement:
--   Given the noise $(B,\theta)$, number the excursions of $B$ away from $0$ by the natural numbers and let $m(t)$ be the number of the excursion straddling $t$. The **spider process** (Walsh Brownian motion on $n$ rays) is
--   $$S_t=\theta_{m(t)}\,|B_t|\in\mathbb C ,$$
--   so that during each excursion the spider moves along one ray, chosen uniformly and independently, at distance $|B_t|$ from the origin.
--
--   For $u\in[0,\infty]$ the **trajectory** is $T_u=\{S_s:0\le s\le u\}$ (1.1). The **rib** on ray $R_k$ is the length $|T_u\cap R_k|$ of the part of that ray visited by time $u$, and for $n\ge2$ the **diameter in the British rail metric** is (1.5)
--   $$D_u=\max\{|T_u\cap R_j|+|T_u\cap R_k| : j\ne k\},$$
--   the sum of the two longest ribs on distinct rays.
--
--   The filtration generated by the spider is $\mathcal F_t=\sigma(S_s: s\le t)$, and a **stopping time of $S$** is a random time $\tau:\Omega\to[0,\infty]$ with $\{\tau\le t\}\in\mathcal F_t$ for all $t\ge 0$.
--
--   These are the objects of inequality (1.6), $\mathbb E D_\tau\le C_n\sqrt{\mathbb E\tau}$.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Excursions are numbered by the first rational time (in a fixed enumeration of the nonnegative rationals) that lies inside the excursion; the paper leaves the order unspecified and any such numbering gives the same law. The rib length is the Lebesgue measure of $\{r\ge0: r e^{2\pi ik/n}\in T_u\}$, valued in $[0,\infty]$. The filtration is the raw natural filtration (not completed); stopping times use the convention $\{\tau\le t\}$.
-- source:
--   Bednarz–Ernst–Osękowski, On the Diameter of the Stopped Spider Process, arXiv:2105.15202v2, §1, pp.1–3: definition of S (p.1), (1.1)–(1.2) (p.2), (1.5) (p.3), stopping times of S (p.2)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_SpiderDiameter_Noise

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory
open scoped NNReal ENNReal
set_option warn.classDefReducibility false

namespace SpiderDiameter

/-! A family of tightly coupled definitions: the spider process of §1, its trajectory, ribs and
British-rail diameter, and its natural filtration and stopping times. -/

/-- An enumeration of the nonnegative rationals (negative rationals are sent to `0`), used to
number the excursions of a path. Every `q ∈ ℚ≥0` is a value. -/
noncomputable def ratEnum (k : ℕ) : ℝ≥0 :=
  Real.toNNReal ((Denumerable.ofNat ℚ k : ℚ) : ℝ)

/-- **Excursion number.** For a path `W` and a time `t` with `W t ≠ 0`, the number of the
excursion of `W` away from `0` that straddles `t`: the least `k` such that the rational time
`ratEnum k` lies in the same excursion as `t`, i.e. `W` has no zero on the closed interval between
`ratEnum k` and `t`. (When no such `k` exists, in particular when `W t = 0`, the value is `0`;
it is then never used.)

Bednarz–Ernst–Osękowski, arXiv:2105.15202v2, §1, p.1: "The set of excursions is countable and
hence it can be ordered by the set of natural numbers. … m(t) is the number of the excursion of B
which straddles t".

**Formalization Note.** The paper leaves the ordering of the excursions unspecified; this is one
concrete ordering, by the first rational time (in the enumeration `ratEnum`) inside the
excursion. Distinct excursions get distinct numbers and the numbering is a function of the path,
so the signs `θ_{m(·)}` attached to the excursions are i.i.d. uniform and independent of `B`,
which is all that the definition of the spider uses. -/
noncomputable def excIndex (W : ℝ≥0 → ℝ) (t : ℝ≥0) : ℕ := by
  classical
  exact if h : ∃ k, ∀ s ∈ Set.uIcc (ratEnum k) t, W s ≠ 0 then Nat.find h else 0

/-- **The spider process** `S_t = θ_{m(t)} |B_t|` as a point of the plane `ℂ`.
Bednarz–Ernst–Osękowski, arXiv:2105.15202v2, §1, p.1: "The spider process S is then given by
S_t = θ_{m(t)}|B_t|, where m(t) is the number of the excursion of B which straddles t".

**Formalization Note.** The complex sign `θ_m` is `rayDir n (θ m)` (ray index `k : Fin n` ↔ the
paper's `k + 1`); `m(t)` is `excIndex` of the path of `B`. When `B_t = 0`, `S_t = 0` whatever
the index. -/
noncomputable def spider {n : ℕ} {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] {P : Measure Ω}
    (N : SpiderNoise n P) (t : ℝ≥0) (ω : Ω) : ℂ :=
  ((|N.B t ω| : ℝ) : ℂ) * rayDir n (N.θ (excIndex (fun s => N.B s ω) t) ω : ℕ)

/-- **Trajectory up to time `u`**, `T_u(ω) = {S_s(ω) : 0 ≤ s ≤ u}`.
Bednarz–Ernst–Osękowski, arXiv:2105.15202v2, eq. (1.1), p.2.

**Formalization Note.** The time `u` ranges over `[0, ∞]` so that the trajectory can be evaluated
at a stopping time; `T_∞` is the whole path (this case only occurs on a null set for integrable
stopping times). -/
def trajectory {n : ℕ} {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] {P : Measure Ω}
    (N : SpiderNoise n P) (u : ℝ≥0∞) (ω : Ω) : Set ℂ :=
  {w | ∃ s : ℝ≥0, (s : ℝ≥0∞) ≤ u ∧ spider N s ω = w}

/-- **The rib on the ray `k`**, `|T_u(ω) ∩ R_k|`: the length (Lebesgue measure) of the set of
distances `r ≥ 0` such that the point at distance `r` on the ray `k` has been visited by time `u`.
Bednarz–Ernst–Osękowski, arXiv:2105.15202v2, (1.2) and the sentence after it, p.2 ("the 'ribs'
of S"); ray `R_k` of p.1.

**Formalization Note.** `|·|` is read as the one-dimensional length of the subset
`T_u(ω) ∩ R_k` of the ray, which is the image of `{r ≥ 0 : r e^{2πi(k+1)/n} ∈ T_u(ω)}` under the
isometry `r ↦ r e^{2πi(k+1)/n}`; the value is in `[0, ∞]`. -/
noncomputable def rib {n : ℕ} {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] {P : Measure Ω}
    (N : SpiderNoise n P) (k : Fin n) (u : ℝ≥0∞) (ω : Ω) : ℝ≥0∞ :=
  volume {r : ℝ | 0 ≤ r ∧ (r : ℂ) * rayDir n k ∈ trajectory N u ω}

/-- **The diameter in the British rail metric**, for `n ≥ 2`:
`D_u(ω) = max{|T_u(ω) ∩ R_j| + |T_u(ω) ∩ R_k| : j, k ∈ {1, …, n}, j ≠ k}`.
Bednarz–Ernst–Osękowski, arXiv:2105.15202v2, eq. (1.5), p.3.

**Formalization Note.** The maximum over the finitely many pairs of distinct rays is a supremum in
`[0, ∞]`. -/
noncomputable def diam {n : ℕ} {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] {P : Measure Ω}
    (N : SpiderNoise n P) (u : ℝ≥0∞) (ω : Ω) : ℝ≥0∞ :=
  ⨆ (j : Fin n) (k : Fin n) (_ : j ≠ k), rib N j u ω + rib N k u ω

/-- **The filtration generated by the spider process**, `𝓕_t = σ(S_s : 0 ≤ s ≤ t)`.
Bednarz–Ernst–Osękowski, arXiv:2105.15202v2, p.2: "stopping time τ of S (i.e., measurable with
respect to the filtration generated by the spider process)".

**Formalization Note.** Written as a family of σ-algebras (the raw natural filtration, neither
completed nor made right-continuous); `ℂ` carries its Borel σ-algebra. -/
noncomputable def spiderSigma {n : ℕ} {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] {P : Measure Ω}
    (N : SpiderNoise n P) (t : ℝ≥0) : MeasurableSpace Ω :=
  ⨆ (s : ℝ≥0) (_ : s ≤ t), MeasurableSpace.comap (spider N s) inferInstance

/-- **Stopping time of the spider process**: a random time `τ : Ω → [0, ∞]` with
`{τ ≤ t} ∈ σ(S_s : s ≤ t)` for every `t ≥ 0`.
Bednarz–Ernst–Osękowski, arXiv:2105.15202v2, p.2.

**Formalization Note.** `[0, ∞]` is `ℝ≥0∞`; the convention is `{τ ≤ t}` (Mathlib's
`IsStoppingTime`), not the optional-time convention `{τ < t}`. -/
def IsSpiderStoppingTime {n : ℕ} {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] {P : Measure Ω}
    (N : SpiderNoise n P) (τ : Ω → ℝ≥0∞) : Prop :=
  ∀ t : ℝ≥0, MeasurableSet[spiderSigma N t] {ω | τ ω ≤ (t : ℝ≥0∞)}

end SpiderDiameter


